f0d8a1bf89febbed123a10608dd7c7ff6456d019cbd2fddf856956","restart_test.go":"85cc774404404149ad1758c50019a36e03ae3abf2a5fea082eb3203f1e46d1ba","worker_test.go":"280f5e430ae63b13f13641512408b69d1d154c0a9b1dee2b35bdc281c3349da1"},"frozen_requirements":{"ack_to_completion_seconds":30,"combined_cycles":10,"cycles_each_pool_client_listener":30,"hold_ms":200,"new_ids_per_recovery":1000,"new_ids_per_second":100,"recovery_seconds":5,"restart_at_s":60,"warmup_s":30,"window_s":120},"interpretations":["S6 'listener' is the HTTP listener on the same loopback address; River's LISTEN connection is replaced only by client/pool recreation.","pool: both pgx pools (caller and trusted-jobs) are closed and recreated, which necessarily recreates the River clients bound to them (riverpgxv5 binds a concrete pool). client: both River clients (insert and worker) are recreated on unchanged pools. combined: listener stop, pools+clients recreation, listener start.","Order: pool arm, client arm, listener arm, then combined; each arm has its own fixture (fresh schema, roles and ledgers) and every cycle its own ledger and ID prefix; IDs persist across same-ID retries.","Each cycle: warm-up then window with arrivals running throughout at 100/s; the restart request is at warm-up+window/2 (measured t=60 s); the hold flag is raised 200 ms before it so HTTP transactions and worker handlers started in that interval are in flight at the request.","S4 operation timeout: 5 s from scheduled arrival including same-ID retries (100 ms backoff). A request not acknowledged by then is recorded as an operation timeout; it stays in the accounting and is never retried afterwards.","Recovery time: from the restart request to the earliest durable completion (result row plus River completed state, read by SQL) of an ID whose client ACK carries the epoch published only by the recreated resources.","Prior acknowledgements: every ID acknowledged before recovery completed must be durably complete within 30 s of its own ACK and no later than 30 s after the recovery instant.","1,000 new IDs: the first RecoveryIDs IDs scheduled at or after the recovery instant must each be acknowledged on the first attempt with no failed attempt of any class, and complete within 30 s of their ACK. Arrival start lag over 50 ms for any of them fails the cycle.","Ack-to-completion is measured from the client-observed 2xx to the first SQL observation of completion (50 ms poll plus a final sweep), so it overestimates by at most the poll interval; completion seen before the ACK counts as 0.","Duplicate logical result: more than one job or one result row per idempotency ID. Duplicate attempts (HTTP retries, River retries/errors, handler redeliveries, replays finding the result present) are reported, not failed; only duplicate logical results are zero-bound.","At most 32 concurrent requests is enforced structurally by a gate in front of every HTTP attempt (a request that finds no free slot waits inside its own 5 s operation timeout and its latency from the scheduled instant; each such wait is reported as concurrency_cap_deferred_attempts) and the in-flight maximum is still observed independently; an eight-connection pool per role is applied. GOMAXPROCS must be 8; race-instrumented runs are labelled not frozen-scale.","Orphans: failed handler drain, StopAndCancel, pool close or listener shutdown within 5 s, plus live pool/client/listener counts and idle-in-transaction sessions of the fixture login role after each cycle.","Section 10: per arm a warmed baseline (after warm-up traffic, drain, quiescence and two GCs), checkpoints after cycles 0/10/20/30 (combined 0/10; reduced arms 0 and the last cycle) taken between cycles, and a teardown check against the pre-application harness baseline. Goroutines are compared per owner/role/generation/entry, descriptors per class, entries per generation, heap against max(1 MiB, 5%); pool idle/total connections and their PostgreSQL sockets/sessions are bounded by exact relations, not by baseline equality. Label: passed only at frozen scale with everything taken, otherwise partially evaluated (reduced) or not evaluated.","Resource owner: kernel (S0WORKERS_RESTART_OWNER=kernel). The pgx pools, the River clients and the HTTP listener are created inside Context.Effect by three fibers (pool, client, listener) built by kernel.New and mounted on the root; the disposers close them; each provides a typed key and the dependants Need it. This is the criteria's section 6 'rerun with resources owned by the final kernel's real effects and actual lease/quiesce behaviour'.","A restart is Root.Batch(UpdateOp(fiber)...) then Root.Settle, never a manual close and recreate: pool restarts the pool fiber, client the client fiber, listener the listener fiber, combined all three in one Batch. Dependants unload and return by availability alone (a Need of a provision that is withdrawn); the harness names no order. Resource recreation time is the Settle return; recovery is judged by the unchanged readiness check.","Leases: every HTTP handler holds Context.Enter leases on the listener, client and pool fibers, every River job on the client and pool fibers, for the whole request or job; the 200 ms hold is taken inside them, so the restart request meets open leases. A refused lease is a 503 (HTTP) or a snooze (job). The kernel's record (Inspect, Events) is read after every Settle: UNLOADING then tracked goroutines done then dispose-effect then ACTIVE, no deadline event, no fiber leaked or carrying a diagnostic, Settle nil, and no disposer ever ran with a lease open.","The kernel's Config values (three deadlines, five capacities) are fixture data, printed in the report config as kernel_config; each deadline is below the gate's 5 s recovery budget."],"limits":["Loopback synthetic HTTP in one process; no production ingress, proxy or TLS. Resources are owned by three kernel fibers of a kernel.New root with no binding maps and open keys; no other plugin, hook, contribution or profile composition is exercised.","The kernel's event ledger carries no lease events and no timestamps: that a drain waited for open leases is shown by the ledger order of UNLOADING, tracked goroutines done, first dispose-effect, ACTIVE, plus the harness's own lease counters read by the disposers (none may find an open lease).","Service values are handles into harness tables: the kernel keeps provision values in its event ledger until EventWindow events pass, which would retain a disposed pool or client.","Listener restart uses graceful Shutdown: in-flight requests finish; abrupt connection loss and ambiguous commits are covered only by the separate smoke controls.","Trusted jobs role sees all tenants and the original fixture authority FAIL is unchanged; this gate does not repair or test authority.","Durable completion is observed by polling SQL, not by a push signal; resolution is the 50 ms poll.","No database crash, power loss or exactly-once delivery claim; result rows are idempotent per job, at-least-once handler attempts are allowed.","S10 resource/leak accounting is enforced for what exists in this probe (see section10 and restart-README.md 'Section 10 requirements'); the kernel's own goroutines (owner loop, observer dispatcher, tracked goroutines) are declared owners, and the kernel's fibers, provisions and effect ledger are judged per restart by the kernel_* bounds. goroutines_after_cycle and open_fds_after_cycle remain unbounded raw observations."],"section10_label":"passed","section10":{"label":"passed","rules":["Warmed baseline: after the application is up and warm-up traffic (at least 3 s at 100/s, the configured warm-up otherwise) has run, drained, quiesced and two GCs.","Checkpoints: after cycle 0 (no restart yet; a control that measurement itself is stable) and after cycles 10, 20, 30 of a 30-cycle arm, 0 and 10 of the combined arm; plus the last cycle of a reduced arm when it is not one of those, so a state after a restart is always evaluated.","Every checkpoint is taken between cycles after drain, quiescence of worker and insert clients (River jobs finished, no request or held transaction in flight, delivery observer queue empty, idle HTTP connections closed), a 250 ms settle, then two GCs.","A violating snapshot is re-taken after 250 ms up to 8 times (transient closing sockets, River's own 5 ms fetch poll); a persistent leak fails on the last attempt, a clean one never waits.","Labels: 'passed' only for a frozen-scale run with every required checkpoint and the teardown check; 'partially evaluated (reduced)' for any reduced run (cycles, windows, race instrumentation, GOMAXPROCS); 'not evaluated' when no baseline was taken or the report has no section 10 field."],"arms":[{"resource_owner":"kernel","arm":"pool","label":"passed","cycles_planned":30,"required_checkpoint_cycles":[0,10,20,30],"scheduled_checkpoint_cycles":[0,10,20,30],"taken_checkpoint_cycles":[0,10,20,30],"declared_background_set":{"goroutine_owners":["go-runtime","net/http-server","pgx-pool","river-client","harness-coordinator","load-generator","kernel"],"background_goroutines_per_live_generation":{"http-server":[{"owner":"kernel","entry":"kernel.(*handle).goTracked.func1","count":2}],"pool-caller":[{"owner":"pgx-pool","entry":"pgx/pgxpool.NewWithConfig.func5","count":1}],"pool-worker":[{"owner":"pgx-pool","entry":"pgx/pgxpool.NewWithConfig.func5","count":1}],"river-worker":[{"owner":"river-client","entry":"river.(*Client[...]).Start.func3","count":1},{"owner":"river-client","entry":"river.(*Client[...]).logStatsLoop.func1","count":1},{"owner":"river-client","entry":"river.(*producer).StartWorkContext.func4","count":1},{"owner":"river-client","entry":"river.(*producer).fetchPollLoop","count":1},{"owner":"river-client","entry":"river.(*producer).heartbeatLogLoop","count":1},{"owner":"river-client","entry":"river.(*producer).pollForJobCancellations","count":1},{"owner":"river-client","entry":"river.(*producer).pollForSettingChanges","count":1},{"o